{
  "coreName": "xemu",
  "systemId": "XBOX",
  "author": "Matt Borgerson and the xemu contributors; QEMU is the work of Fabrice Bellard and the QEMU team",
  "url": "https://github.com/xemu-project/xemu",
  "romFile": "dvd",
  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    512,
    16,
    128,
    256,
    2048
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap - the layout run-wbx.c proved. QEMU is enormous: glib wants a fat heap, the TCG translation cache and the machine's 64-128MB of RAM live in the mmap arena, and the COW overlays for the HDD and DVD grow there too.",
  "video": {
    "_comment": "buffer CAPACITY; the live size comes from GetVideoWidth/Height per frame. The machine's common mode is 640x480; the GL renderer can scan out up to 1080i and the GPU-bridged upscaler more, so the buffer takes 1920x1080. The vsync fraction is EXACTLY the driver's frame schedule: 60000/1001 (NTSC, what real Xbox video hardware scans out), walked as absolute deadlines of 50,050,000ns per three frames since the quantum is not a whole nanosecond - see VBLANK_3FRAMES_NS in the driver.",
    "width": 1920,
    "height": 1080,
    "virtualWidth": 640,
    "virtualHeight": 480,
    "vsyncNumerator": 60000,
    "vsyncDenominator": 1001,
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra"
  },
  "audio": {
    "_comment": "the APU monitor's 48kHz stereo stream; ~800 pairs arrive per 60Hz frame and the accumulator holds 16384 for frames the frontend skips draining.",
    "rate": 48000,
    "samplesPerFrame": 16384,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "extensions": {
    ".iso": "XBOX",
    ".xiso": "XBOX"
  },
  "input": {
    "name": "Xbox Controller",
    "_comment": "Four Duke pads, always plugged - the machine's USB topology is part of the machine, so the ports do not come and go with settings. The button list is in the core's packed-bit order (the xemu CONTROLLER_BUTTON enum) and the axes in SetAxis index order (LTrig, RTrig, LStick X/Y, RStick X/Y per port); reordering either breaks the wire format. The triggers are analog 0..32767; the face buttons the hardware reports as pressure are declared digital, which is how nearly every game reads them.",
